From: PS3 Custom Toolchain
Subject: [PATCH] lv2: exit() runs the calling thread's thread_local destructors first

With GCC 16's posix thread model, libstdc++ registers each thread_local
with a non-trivial destructor through __cxa_thread_atexit_impl, which
librt provides; a worker thread's list runs in librt's thread-exit
cleanup.  For the thread that calls exit() (normally the main thread,
returning from main), its thread_locals must die before the atexit
handlers and static destructors run, as the C++ standard orders them:
those handlers include libstdc++'s run of the main thread's
notify_all_at_thread_exit list.  exit() now calls librt's
__lv2_tls_dtors_run first, through a weak reference, so a program without
thread_locals links as before.  _exit and abort are unchanged.

diff --git a/newlib/libc/stdlib/exit.c b/newlib/libc/stdlib/exit.c
index 9b7bd51..1ea7a15 100644
--- a/newlib/libc/stdlib/exit.c
+++ b/newlib/libc/stdlib/exit.c
@@ -49,9 +49,22 @@ Supporting OS subroutines required: <<_exit>>.
  * Exit, flushing stdio buffers if necessary.
  */
 
+#ifdef __lv2ppu__
+/* librt: runs the calling thread's thread_local destructors.  Weak: a program
+   that never registered one need not link it.  */
+extern void __lv2_tls_dtors_run (void) __attribute__((weak));
+#endif
+
 void
 exit (int code)
 {
+#ifdef __lv2ppu__
+  /* The exiting thread's thread_locals die before the atexit handlers and
+     static destructors run, as the C++ standard orders them (glibc does the
+     same).  _exit and abort skip both.  */
+  if (__lv2_tls_dtors_run)
+    __lv2_tls_dtors_run ();
+#endif
 #ifdef _LITE_EXIT
   /* Refer to comments in __atexit.c for more details of lite exit.  */
   void __call_exitprocs (int, void *) __attribute__((weak));
